ext says \"some first-grade students are the children of migrant workers,\" which could mean that some are, but not necessarily all. So, D is incorrect because it's making a claim that some are not, which isn't supported by the context.\n\nSo, going back, B is correct because the context says some first-grade students are children of migrant workers, and since third-grade students are all registered, but the migrant worker children aren't, so some of the registered students must be third-grade. So, B is correct.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused because I thought D was correct, but now I'm seeing that D is incorrect because the context doesn't say all first-grade students are children of migrant workers, only some.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is B.\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The context states that all third-grade students are registered in the city, implying that some students with registration are third-grade.\n2. Option B states that some children of migrant workers are third-grade students, which aligns with the information that some first-grade migrant children exist, and since third-grade students are registered, B is correct.\n3. Option D incorrectly assumes all first-grade students are migrant children, which isn't supported as the context only mentions some, not all.\n\nAnswer: B",
