messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nThe literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, based on this, which of the following options is correct?\n# Options:\nA) It's national, it's world\nB) If it is not national, it is not world\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world\nD) Only the world is national", '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to solve this logical reasoning problem based on the given context.Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says: "The literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.".\n\nHmm, that\'s a bit dense.Let me break it down.It\'s talking about Mo Yan\'s literary works and how they\'ve influenced both Chinese vernacular (which I think means everyday language or culture) and the international literary world.It mentions that this achievement proves that "only the national is the world.".\n\nSo, the key point here is that Mo Yan\'s work has shown that national identity is what connects to the world.In other words, the national aspect is what makes something (in this case, Mo Yan\'s work) recognized internationally.\n\nNow, looking at the question: Based on the above information, which of the following options is correct?The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: "It\'s national, it\'s world."This seems to suggest that if something is national, then it\'s also world.But I\'m not sure if that\'s exactly what the context is saying.The context is more about how Mo Yan\'s national work is recognized internationally, so maybe this is a possible interpretation, but I\'m not certain.\n\nOption B: "If it is not national, it is not world."This is a bit tricky.It\'s a contrapositive of the statement "Only national is world."Wait, no, actually, "only the national is the world" would mean that something can only be considered world-class if it\'s national.So, if it\'s not national, it can\'t be world.That seems to align with option B.\n\nOption C: "As long as the nation is, it is the world."I have no idea what I\'m talking about and I\'m completely unsure of my understanding.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
