ffects the outcome (ease of meeting the requirement). So, the argument is that when you have more requirements (many guests), it's harder to meet them, so you need more guards or more active ingredients.\n\nOption C is about the number of guards needed based on size, which is a proportional relationship, but the context is about the number of guests and the number of active ingredients. So, the structure is similar in that it's about the number of something (guests or guards) and how it affects the outcome.\n\nWait, but the example in the context is about the difficulty of satisfying many guests versus one guest, which is about the number of guests (requirements) and the ease of meeting them. The argument is that it's easier to satisfy one guest because you can adjust, but difficult to satisfy many.\n\nOption C is about the number of guards needed based on the size of the supermarket, which is a proportional relationship. So, if a supermarket needs three guards, it's a big one. So, the structure is about the number of guards (one vs. three) and the size (small vs. big). So, it's similar to the number of active ingredients (one vs. many) and the number of guests (one vs. many).\n\nTherefore, Option C is similar in structure to the argument because it's about the number of guards needed based on size, which is a proportional relationship, just like the number of active ingredients needed based on the number of guests.\n\nWait, but the context isn't about the number of active ingredients but the number of guests. So, the structure is about the number of requirements (guests) and how it affects the outcome (ease of meeting the requirement). Option C is about the number of guards (number of requirements) and the size (which determines the number of guards needed). So, it's similar in that it's about the number of something needed based on another factor.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is Option C.\n</think>\n\nThe correct answer is C.\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe argument in the context compares standard and herbal antibiotics, noting that the latter, with more active ingredients, are more effective against new drug-resistant bacteria. This is because meeting the requirement of satisfying many guests (like many bacteria) is harder, whereas satisfying one guest (a single bacteria) is easier.\n\nOption C discusses a supermarket needing a certain number of security guards based on size. It's similar because it relates the number of guards (a requirement) to the size of the supermarket, which determines how many guards are needed. Just like the context relates the number of active ingredients (a requirement) to the type of antibiotic, Option C relates the number of guards (a requirement) to the size of the store (determining guard numbers). Both structures compare the number of requirements to an outcome, mirroring the argument's focus on the number of requirements affecting effectiveness.",
