messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nConfirmation bias refers to the fact that once a belief is established, people try to find examples that are consistent with it and ignore those that are not.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, based on the above definition, which of the following is the most likely the confirmation bias?\n# Options:\nA) Despite being told that all kimchi ingredients in the pickle jar are made at the same time, chef xiao huang still thinks that using the kimchi in the yellow jar to cook fish-flavored pork will be more delicious.\nB) The stock manager told a client named xiaoming that a stock would go up while telling other clients behind his back that the stock would go down. as a result, the stock went up, so xiaoming trusted the manager very much.\nC) Xiaogang was obsessed with the lottery because he thought it would be a windfall one day. though he never won the lottery, he idled around all day and even took out a loan to buy a lottery ticket.\nD) Xiaodong often worried when he heard some so called prophet conclude that he was going to be in a car accident. eventually, after a brush with another vehicle, xiaodong trusted the prophet more.', "<think>\n\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is the best example of confirmation bias based on the given definition.Let me start by understanding what confirmation bias is.The context says that confirmation bias is when people believe a certain belief and then seek out information that confirms it while ignoring contradictory evidence.\n\nSo, the key points are: once a belief is established, people look for supporting evidence and ignore conflicting information.\n\nNow, let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Despite being told that all kimchi ingredients in the pickle jar are made at the same time, chef xiao huang still thinks that using the kimchi in the yellow jar to cook fish-flavored pork will be more delicious.Hmm, this seems to be about the belief that the yellow jar's kimchi is better for cooking.But the context given is about ignoring contradictory information.Here, the chef was told all jars are made at the same time, so maybe he's ignoring that fact?Or is he choosing the yellow jar because he thinks it's better, even though the info says they're all same?I'm not sure if this is about confirmation bias because it's more about personal preference than seeking out conflicting info.\n\nOption B: The stock manager told a client named xiaoming that a stock would go up while telling other clients behind his back that the stock would go down.As a result, the stock went up, so xiaoming trusted the manager.Wait, so the manager told xiaoming it would go up, and the stock did go up.So xiaoming trusted the manager because the stock moved as he thought.But the manager also told other clients it would go down, which didn't happen.So xiaoming only heard the positive info, and trusted the manager.This seems like confirmation bias because xiaoming only focused on the info that supported his belief (stock goes up) and ignored the contradictory info (other clients were told it would go down, but stock went up).So this fits the definition.\nOption A: Xiaogang was uninterested in the lottery because he believed it would never bring him any luck.He always won, so he stayed productive and saved money to invest in his future.This is more about disinterest and wise financial decisions, not about seeking confirmation.It's more about skepticism in a belief without seeking evidence, but not necessarily ignoring contradictory info.\n\nOption E: Xiaodong doubted a prophet more after a car accident because the prophet predicted it.So he relaxed before, then after the accident, distrusted the prophet.This seems more like he was unaffected by the prophecy, but not necessarily ignoring contradictory info.It's more about doubt and unpredictability rather than seeking confirmation.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option F is the best example of confirmation bias because the person only considered the information that supported their belief and ignored the contradictory information given to others.. Therefore, my final option is: "]] 34%|###3      | 203/600 [36:05<07:37,  1.15s/it]
