re being sanctioned, but it doesn't say anything about illegal acts that are not criminal. So, the assertion doesn't imply that all illegal acts are not being sanctioned. Therefore, A is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is about what must be true based on the given information. So, perhaps I'm overcomplicating.\n\nLet me try to visualize this with sets.\n\nLet me draw a Venn diagram.\n\n- Let's say the set of Criminal Acts (CA) is entirely within the set of Actions Sanctioned by Criminal Law (SCL). So, every CA is an SCL.\n\n- Some Illegal Acts (IA) are CA. So, the IA set overlaps with CA.\n\n- Huang Tao's acts are IA.\n\nSo, from this, we can say that some IA are CA, which are SCL. Therefore, some IA are SCL. So, that would mean that some of Huang Tao's acts, if they are IA and CA, would be SCL. But it's possible that his acts are IA but not CA, so they might not be SCL.\n\nBut the question is, which must be true. So, let's look at the options again.\n\nOption A: No illegal acts are SCL. But from the context, only some illegal acts are CA, which are SCL. So, some illegal acts are SCL, so A is false.\n\nOption B: Huang Tao's behavior is not a crime. Well, being an illegal act doesn't necessarily mean it's a crime. The context doesn't define what constitutes a crime beyond being a CA. So, B is not necessarily true.\n\nOption C: His actions will be SCL. As discussed, it's possible that his actions are CA, so they would be SCL, but it's also possible that they are IA but not CA, so not SCL. Therefore, C is not necessarily true.\n\nOption D: All SCL are CA. The context says all CA are SCL, but it doesn't say that all SCL are CA. So, D is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but I'm confused because none of the options seem to be necessarily true. But that can't be right because the question is asking which must be true.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in analyzing option C. Let me think again.\n\nThe context says some IA are CA, and all CA are SCL. So, some IA are SCL. Therefore, if Huang Tao's acts are IA, then it's possible that they are among those IA that are CA, hence SCL. But it's also possible that his acts are IA but not CA, so not SCL. Therefore, we can't conclude that his actions are definitely SCL. So, C is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is about what must be true. So, perhaps none of the options are necessarily true, but that can't be the case because the question expects one of them to be correct.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing something. Let me try to re-express the statements in logical terms.\n\nLet me denote:\n\nCA: Criminal Act\n\nIA: Illegal Act\n\nSCL: Sanctioned by Criminal Law\n\nGiven:\n\n1. All CA are SCL. So, CA \u2192 SCL.\n\n2. Some IA are CA. So, \u2203x (IA(x) \u2227 CA(x)).\n\n3. IA_huang = Huang Tao's acts are IA.\n\nWe need to find which of the options must be true.\n\nOption A: No IA are SCL. That would mean IA \u2192 \u00acSCL. But from the premises, we know that some IA are CA, and CA \u2192 SCL. Therefore, those IA that are CA are SCL. So, A is false because some IA are SCL.\n\nOption B: Huang's behavior is not a crime. Being an IA doesn't necessarily mean it's a crime. The context doesn't define what constitutes a crime beyond being a CA. So, B is not necessarily true.\n\nOption C: His actions will be SCL. As discussed, it's possible that his actions are CA, so SCL, but it's also possible that they are IA but not CA, so not SCL. Therefore, C is not necessarily true.\n\nOption D: All SCL are CA. The premises only state that all CA are SCL, not the converse. So, D is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which must be true. So, perhaps I'm missing something. Let me think again.\n\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in interpreting the statements. Let me re-express them.\n\n1. All CA are SCL. So, if something is a CA, then it's SCL.\n\n2. Some IA are CA. So, there exists at least one IA that is a CA.\n\n3. IA_huang is true.\n\nSo, from 2 and 3, we can conclude that at least one IA (Huang's) is a CA, and since all CA are SCL, then at least one IA is SCL. Therefore, some IA are SCL.\n\nBut the options are about what must be true.\n\nOption C says that Huang's actions will be SCL. But from the premises, we only know that some IA are CA, and that CA are SCL. So, it's possible that Huang's actions are among those IA that are CA, hence SCL. But it's also possible that his actions are IA but not CA, so not SCL. Therefore, we can't conclude that his actions are definitely SCL. So, C is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is about what must be true. So, perhaps none of the options are necessarily true, but that can't be the case because the question expects one of them to be correct.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me try to think differently.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options must be true based on the given premises.\n\nGiven that all CA are SCL, and some IA are CA, and IA_huang is true.\n\nSo, IA_huang is true, meaning his actions are IA.\n\nNow, since some IA are CA, and CA are SCL, then some IA are SCL. Therefore, it's possible that Huang's actions are among those IA that are CA, hence SCL. But it's also possible that his actions are IA but not CA, so not SCL.\n\nTherefore, we can't conclude that his actions are definitely SCL. So, C is not necessarily true.\n\nSimilarly, A says no IA are SCL, but we know that some IA are CA, which are SCL, so A is false.\n\nB says Huang's behavior is not a crime. But being an IA doesn't necessarily mean it's a crime. The context doesn't define what constitutes a crime beyond being a CA. So, B is not necessarily true.\n\nD says all SCL are CA. The premises only state that all CA are SCL, not the converse. So, D is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which must be true. So, perhaps none of the options are necessarily true, but that can't be the case because the question expects one of them to be correct.\n\nWait, maybe I'm missing somet