messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nWe cannot simply think that the editors of the lexicon are wrong. They will not make changes to the usage of the vocabulary randomly, and must have been carefully researched. However, the lexicographers cannot ignore a basic fact and the basic requirements derived from it : Spoken and written languages are commonly used by the broad masses of the people and have a very broad social nature. Therefore, the standardization of spoken and written languages cannot be carried out in the ivory tower, but must follow the mass line.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is the basic requirements in this passage?\n# Options:\nA) The lexicographer cannot change the usage of the vocabulary at will\nB) The lexicographer should be familiar with the specific process of lexicography\nC) Standardization of spoken and written languages must serve the broad masses of people\nD) The standardization of spoken and written language should be decided by the broad masses of people', "<think>\n.\nOkay, so I need to figure out the answer to this question based on the given passage.Let me read through the context again carefully.\n\nThe passage starts by saying that lexicographers aren't just randomly changing vocabulary; they must have done thorough research.So, the first point is that lexicographers make changes carefully, not randomly.That relates to option A, which says the lexicographer cannot change the usage of the vocabulary at will.That seems to fit because the passage emphasizes careful research before any changes.\n\nNext, the passage mentions that spoken and written languages are used by the broad masses and have a broad social nature.Hence, standardization can easily take place in an ivory tower and should precede the mass line. Therefore, the basic requirements here are not about standardization benefiting the masses. Considering the options, option C mentions standardization should not prioritize the broad masses, which contradicts this point.\n\nOption D implies that standardization should not be influenced by the masses, yet the passage clearly states that decisions are made by the masses; it asserts standardization must follow the mass line, indicating the masses make the final decisions.\n\nOption B discusses ignorance of the specific process of lexicography, and while the passage doesn't specifically address the process, it does emphasize that changes are haphazardly researched.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
