s about the necessity of a third party (the doctor) providing the warning because people can't easily deceive themselves. So the assumption is that people generally find it hard to deceive themselves, which is option B.\n\nWait, but the context says that such strategies involve deception, so individuals cannot easily adopt them unless a third party provides the warning. So the assumption is that people can't easily deceive themselves, so they need the doctor's warning. Therefore, the assumption is that people generally do not find it easy to deceive themselves, which is option B.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the assumption is that a doctor is justified in deceiving a patient, which is option C.\n\nI'm a bit torn between B and C. Let me think again.\n\nThe argument is that doctors use deception to help people quit smoking, and this can be applied to other habits. But because it's deception, people can't do it themselves unless a third party provides the warning. So the assumption is that people can't easily deceive themselves, so they need the doctor's warning. Therefore, the assumption is that people generally do not find it easy to deceive themselves, which is option B.\n\nYes, that makes sense. So the answer should be B.\n</think>\n\nThe argument depends on the assumption that people generally do not find it easy to deceive themselves, necessitating the role of a third party, such as a doctor, to provide the necessary warnings. \n\nAnswer: B",
