That seems contradictory because if the universe is only a few billion years old, how could gas clouds with carbon exist after that?.\n\nNow, looking at the options:.\n\nA) Primitive planet contains only element hydrogen.Hmm, the context says primitive planets have hydrogen and helium, so A is probably false.\n\nB) Some planets were formed before the age of the universe reached two billion years.Wait, the universe is only a few billion years old, so planets must have formed within that time.If the universe is only a few billion years old, planets couldn't have formed before that.So B might not be correct.\n\nC) The carbon element in the gas cloud later formed parts of some planets.This seems to make sense because the gas clouds with carbon were found after the universe's age, but the carbon was already present in the gas clouds.So maybe the gas clouds were from earlier planets that had formed, and then later, when those planets exploded, the heavier elements were dispersed.Or perhaps the gas clouds themselves were from the early universe, and the carbon was part of the material that eventually formed the planets.So C could be true.The gas clouds with carbon were not formed after the planets, but rather were part of the material that later made up some planets.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
