      "text": "\u00a0D\n</think>\n\nLet's analyze the problem step by step.\n\n**Given:**\n1. There are only **liberal arts** and **science** classes in a high school.\n2. There are **more boys than girls** in the school.\n3. There are **more science classes than liberal arts classes**.\n\n**Question:**\nBased on the above information, which of the following options must be correct?\n\n**Options:**\nA) The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is more than the total number of girls in the liberal arts class.\nB) The total number of boys in the science class is more than the total number of girls in the science class.\nC) The total number of boys in the liberal arts class is higher than that of girls in the science class.\nD) The total number of boys in the science class is more than that of girls in liberal arts class.\n\n**Analysis:**\n\n1. **More Boys Than Girls:**\n   - The total number of boys in all classes combined is greater than the total number of girls.\n\n2. **More Science Classes Than Liberal Arts Classes:**\n   - There are more science classes than liberal arts classes. This means that, on average, each science class might have fewer students than each liberal arts class, or the distribution of students between classes could vary.\n\n3. **Option D:**\n   - **\"The total number of boys in the science class is more than that of girls in liberal arts class.\"**\n   - Given that there are more boys overall and more science classes, it is logical to infer that there must be at least one science class with more boys than there are girls in some liberal arts class. This ensures that the total number of boys across all science classes exceeds the total number of girls across all liberal arts classes.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nOption D must be correct based on the given conditions.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\n\\boxed{D}",
