messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D.\n####\n# Context:\nIf you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is closest to the meaning of the above judgement?\n# Options:\nA) If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors.\nB) Truth can not be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed.\nC) If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed.\nD) To accept the truth, we must reject wrong.', '<think>\n.\nOkay, so I\'m trying to solve this logical reasoning problem.Let me read it carefully.\n\nThe context given is: "If you close all the wrong doors, truth will be shut out."And the question is asking which option is closest to the meaning of this judgement.The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to understand the original statement.It\'s a conditional statement, right?It says that if you close all the wrong doors (let\'s call this action "P"), then the truth will be shut out ("Q").So in logical terms, it\'s P → Q.\n\nNow, I remember that in logic, a conditional statement P → Q is equivalent to its contrapositive, which is ¬Q → ¬P.The contrapositive is logically equivalent, meaning it has the same truth value as the original statement.So if the original is true, the contrapositive is also true, and vice versa.\n\nLooking at the options, I need to see which one matches either the original statement or its contrapositive.\n\nOption A: "If truth is shut out, it must be that you close all the wrong doors."That sounds like Q → P. But in logic, the contrapositive is ¬Q → ¬P, not Q → P. So A is not the contrapositive; it\'s actually the inverse of the original statement, which isn\'t necessarily equivalent.So A might not be correct.\n\nOption B: "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed."Hmm, this is a bit tricky.Let\'s break it down."Unless all the doors of wrong are closed" means that if you don\'t close all the wrong doors, then truth can\'t be shut out.Wait, that sounds like the contrapositive.Because the original is P → Q, so the contrapositive is ¬Q → ¬P, which is "If truth is not shut out, then you did not close all the wrong doors."But B says "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed," which is a bit different.Let me think again."Unless" can sometimes be tricky.It\'s like saying "if not P, then not Q."Wait, no, the contrapositive is ¬Q → ¬P, which is "If truth is not shut out, then you did not close all the wrong doors."So B is phrased as "Truth cannot be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are closed," which is equivalent to "If truth is not shut out, then all the doors of wrong are closed."Wait, that doesn\'t match.Because the contrapositive is ¬Q → ¬P, which is "If truth is not shut out, then you did not close all the wrong doors."So B is actually the inverse of the contrapositive.Hmm, maybe I\'m getting confused here.\n\nOption C: "If the truth is not shut out, then the door to all wrong must not be closed."Option D: "To reject the truth, we must accept wrong."This is quite clear.It suggests that rejecting truth involves accepting wrong paths.But the original statement discusses opening wrong paths leading to truth being revealed.So if you open the wrong paths, truth is revealed.So D is more about accepting wrong paths leading to rejecting truth, which is directly stated.It\'s more of a converse or maybe the inverse.\n\nWait, let me double-check.The original is P → Q, where P is opening all wrong paths, and Q is truth being revealed.So the contrapositive is ¬Q → ¬P, which is "If truth is not revealed, then you did not open all the wrong paths."So C is exactly that.So C is correct.\n\nBut let me make sure I\'m not mixing up the options.Option B says "Truth can be shut out unless all the doors of wrong are open."That translates to "If truth is revealed, then all the doors of wrong are open," which is Q → P. But the original is P → Q, so B is the converse, which isn\'t necessarily true.So B is not correct\n. Therefore, my final option is: ']] 80%|#######9  | 478/600 [1:14:32<03:05,  1.52s/it]
