ompany to benefit society, whether it's through compensation, preventing pollution, donating, or employment, is part of it.\n\nSo all options A, B, C, D are examples of corporate social response.But the question is asking which does NOT belong.So maybe I'm missing something.\n\nWait, maybe option A is not about the company but about others compensating, so it's not part of the company's corporate social response.But the question is about which option does not belong to corporate social response in general, not specifically to the company.\n\nWait, no, the question is about which option does not belong to corporate social response, so all options are examples except one.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating.Let me think again.\n\nThe definition is about enterprises being guided by social ethics to meet common needs.So any action taken by a company to benefit society is part of it.\n\nSo all options A, B, C, D are examples of corporate social response.But the question is asking which does NOT belong.So maybe I'm wrong.\n\nWait, maybe option A is not about the company but about others compensating, so it's not part of the company's corporate social response.But the question is about which option does not belong to corporate social response, so maybe A is the one that doesn't belong because it's about others, not the company itself.\n\nWait, but the question is about which option does not belong to corporate social response, so maybe A is the one that doesn't belong because it's about others, not the company.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the question is about whether the action is taken by the company.Let me check:.\n\nA: Surrounding enterprises pay compensation.So it's the surrounding businesses, not the company causing pollution.So the company is the polluter, and others are compensating.So it's more about the company's pollution and others' response, not the company's own corporate social response.\n\nWait, but the definition is about enterprises being guided by social eth