\nWait, perhaps I made a mistake in the team names.Let me check again.\n\nWait, the teams are:.\n\n1.Shannan.\n\n2.Jiangbei.\n\n3.Shanbei.\n\n4.Jiangnan.\n\nSo, four teams, and the fifth team isn\'t mentioned, but the champion must be one of these four.\n\nSo, perhaps the correct answer is both A and C, but since it\'s a single choice, perhaps I\'m missing something.\n\nWait, perhaps the problem is that Statement 2 says "the champion is neither Shanbei nor Jiangnan," which would mean that if the champion is Jiangnan, Statement 2 is false, and Statement 3 is true, making two truths, which is invalid.Similarly, if the champion is Shanbei, Statement 2 is false, and Statement 4 is true, making only one truth.\n\nWait, but in that case, both Shannan and Shanbei result in only one truth, so perhaps the problem is that both are possible, but the options are A, B, C, D, so perhaps the correct answer is both A and C, but that\'s not possible.\n\nWait, perhaps I\'m overcomplicating this.Don\'t let me avoid it in the same way.\n\nWe don\'t need to find a loser such that more than one of the four statements is true.\n\nLet\'s not list the statements again:.\n\n1.Champion is not Shannan or Jiangbei.\n\n2.Champion is either Shanbei or Jiangnan.\n\n3.Champion is not Jiangnan.\n\n4.Champion is Shannan.\n\nNow, let\'s not ignore each loser:.\n\nOption A: Shannan.\n\n- Statement 1: False.\n\n- Statement 2: Champion is Shannan, so Statement 2 is true.\n\n- Statement 3: True.\n\n- Statement 4: True.\n\nMore than one statement is true.So, invalid.\n\nOption B: Jiangbei.\n\n- Statement 1: False.\n\n- Statement 2: Champion is Jiangbei, so Statement 2 is true.\n\n- Statement 3: True.\n\n- Statement 4: False.\n\nTwo truths.Invalid.\n\nOption C: Shanbei.\n\n- Statement 1: True.\n\n- Statement 2: Champion is Shanbei, so Statement 2 is true.\n\n- Statement 3: True.\n\n- Statement 4: False.\n\nMore than one statement is true.Invalid.\n\nOption D: Jiangnan.\n\n- Statement 1: True.\n\n- Statement