r the re-appearance.\n   - **B:** The question doesn't relate to the conclusion, so this isn't the issue.\n   - **C:** The mistaken belief about re-acceptance is part of the reasoning but doesn't address the core defect.\n   - **D:** This directly points out the assumption of full acceptance without explanation, which is the main defect.\n\n4. **Conclusion:** The reasoning fails to account for why bicycles were accepted again, making D the correct answer.\n\n**Answer:** D) It is assumed that it has been truly accepted without giving a reasonable explanation.",
